          "id": "sec-worked-example-2-contour-integration-with-isolated-poles-via-the-msra-residue-theorem",
          "title": "Worked Example 2: Contour Integration with Isolated Poles via the MSRA Residue Theorem",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Worked Example 2: Contour Integration with Isolated Poles via the MSRA Residue Theorem\n\nTo demonstrate the application of the MSRA Residue Theorem, evaluate the closed contour integral of the aperture rational function \\( \\Psi ( z ) = \\frac { \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } } { z ^ 2 + 1 } \\) along a closed circular contour \\( \\Gamma \\) centered at \\( z = i \\) with radius \\( R = 1 \\) oriented counterclockwise.\n\n. **Identify Poles**: The integrand has isolated poles where the denominator vanishes: \\[ z ^ 2 + 1 = 0 \\implies z _ 1 = i , \\quad z _ 2 = - i \\]\nThe contour \\( \\Gamma \\) (radius 1 centered at \\( i \\)) encloses only the pole \\( z _ 1 = i \\), while \\( z _ 2 = - i \\) lies outside \\( \\text { Int } ( \\Gamma ) \\).\n\n. **Compute MSRA Residue at \\( z _ 1 = i \\)**: Decompose \\( \\Psi ( z ) \\) into partial fraction aperture components: \\[ \\Psi ( z ) = \\frac { \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } } { ( z - i ) ( z + i ) } = \\frac { \\frac { \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } } { z + i } } { z - i } \\]\n\nThe residue at simple pole \\( z _ 1 = i \\) is: \\[ \\text { Res } \\left ( \\Psi , i \\right ) = \\left . ( z - i ) \\Psi ( z ) \\right | _ { z = i } = \\left . \\frac { \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } } { z + i } \\right | _ { z = i } = \\frac { \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } } { 2i } = - \\frac { i } { 2 } \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\]\n\n\n. **Apply the MSRA Residue Theorem**: Substitute the computed residue into the residue sum formula: \\[ \\oint _ \\Gamma \\frac { 1 } { z ^ 2 + 1 } \\, dz = 2 \\pi i \\, \\text { Res } \\left ( \\Psi , i \\right ) = 2 \\pi i \\left ( - \\frac { i } { 2 } \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\right ) = \\pi \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\]\n\n\n. **Direct Parametric Verification**: Parameterize \\( \\Gamma \\) as \\( \\gamma ( t ) = i + r e ^ { i t } \\) for \\( t \\in [ 0 , 2 \\pi ] \\) with \\( r = 1 \\). Then \\( \\gamma ' ( t ) = i e ^ { i t } \\, dt \\). Substituting into the integral: \\[ \\oint _ \\Gamma \\frac { dz } { z ^ 2 + 1 } = \\int _ 0 ^ { 2 \\pi } \\frac { i e ^ { i t } } { ( i + e ^ { i t } ) ^ 2 + 1 } \\, dt = \\int _ 0 ^ { 2 \\pi } \\frac { i e ^ { i t } } { 2i e ^ { i t } + e ^ { 2i t } } \\, dt = \\int _ 0 ^ { 2 \\pi } \\frac { i } { 2i + e ^ { i t } } \\, dt = \\pi \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\]\n\nThis explicitly verifies the MSRA Residue Theorem for rational aperture field functions.\n\n[#chap-the-master-field-equation]"
