          "id": "sec-proof-of-the-fundamental-anti-commutation-law",
          "title": "Proof of the Fundamental Anti-Commutation Law",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Proof of the Fundamental Anti-Commutation Law\n\n[#theorem-cl411-anticommutation]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Fundamental Anti-Commutation Law of Cl(4,1,1)\n====\nFor any two generator basis elements \\( e _ A , e _ B \\in \\{ e _ 1 , e _ 2 , e _ 3 , e _ 4 , e _ + , e _ - \\} \\) in \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\), the fundamental anti-commutation relation holds: \\[ e _ A e _ B + e _ B e _ A = 2 g _ { AB } \\mathbf { 1 } \\]\n\n\n*Proof:*\n. Let \\( u , v \\in V \\). By definition of the ideal quotient \\( I ( Q ) \\), we have \\( ( u + v ) ^ 2 = Q ( u + v ) \\mathbf { 1 } \\).\n\n. Expanding the left-hand side in \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\): \\[ ( u + v ) ^ 2 = ( u + v ) ( u + v ) = u ^ 2 + u v + v u + v ^ 2 = Q ( u ) \\mathbf { 1 } + u v + v u + Q ( v ) \\mathbf { 1 } \\]\n\n\n. Expanding the right-hand side using linearity of the metric bilinear form: \\[ Q ( u + v ) \\mathbf { 1 } = \\left ( Q ( u ) + Q ( v ) + 2 B ( u , v ) \\right ) \\mathbf { 1 } \\]\n\n\n. Equating LHS and RHS and subtracting \\( Q ( u ) \\mathbf { 1 } + Q ( v ) \\mathbf { 1 } \\) from both sides yields: \\[ u v + v u = 2 B ( u , v ) \\mathbf { 1 } \\]\n\n\n. Setting \\( u = e _ A \\) and \\( v = e _ B \\) gives \\( B ( e _ A , e _ B ) = g _ { AB } \\). Thus \\( e _ A e _ B + e _ B e _ A = 2 g _ { AB } \\mathbf { 1 } \\). For distinct orthogonal generators (\\( A \\neq B \\)), \\( g _ { AB } = 0 \\implies e _ A e _ B = - e _ B e _ A \\). For equal generators, \\( e _ A ^ 2 = g _ { AA } \\mathbf { 1 } \\). \\( \\square \\)\n===="
