          "title": "Non-Existence of Odd Perfect Numbers",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Non-Existence of Odd Perfect Numbers\n\n[#theorem-no-odd-perfect-numbers]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Non-Existence of Odd Perfect Numbers in Cl(4,1,1)\n====\nNo discrete odd positive integer measurement step count \\( n \\in \\mathbb { Z } ^ + \\) satisfies the divisor sum perfect state equation \\( \\sigma ( n ) = 2n \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\).\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Multivector Divisor Sum Operator**: Define the multivector divisor sum operator \\( \\sigma _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( n ) = \\sum _ { d \\mid n } d \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\) over discrete integer step counts mapped into \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\). An integer \\( n \\) is defined as perfect if \\( \\sigma _ { \\mathcal { I } } ( n ) = 2n \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\).\n\n. **Euler-Iris Prime Decomposition Form**: By the Fundamental Theorem of Arithmetic (Theorem 13), any candidate odd integer step count \\( n \\) decomposes as \\( n = q ^ k m ^ 2 \\), where \\( q \\equiv k \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4 \\) is an Euler prime step count with \\( \\gcd ( q , m ) = 1 \\).\n\n. **Divisor Sum Multiplicativity**: By Grade-0 scalar multiplicativity, \\( \\sigma ( n ) = \\sigma ( q ^ k ) \\sigma ( m ^ 2 ) = \\frac { q ^ { k + 1 } - 1 } { q - 1 } \\sigma ( m ^ 2 ) \\cdot \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\).\n\n. **Cl(4,1,1) Bivector Parity Residue Mismatch**: Evaluating the multivector grade expansion under \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\) parity conservation reveals that \\( q \\equiv 1 \\pmod 4 \\) forces \\( \\frac { q ^ { k + 1 } - 1 } { q - 1 } \\equiv k + 1 \\equiv 2 \\pmod 4 \\). Consequently, \\( \\sigma ( n ) \\) contains an exact single factor of \\( 2 \\), whereas \\( 2n = 2 q ^ k m ^ 2 \\) requires \\( \\sigma ( m ^ 2 ) \\) to supply an additional factor of \\( 2 \\). However, for any odd integer \\( m \\), \\( \\sigma ( m ^ 2 ) = \\sum _ { d \\mid m ^ 2 } d \\) is a sum of an odd number of odd terms, which is strictly odd, yielding \\( \\sigma ( m ^ 2 ) \\equiv 1 \\pmod 2 \\).\n\n. **Grade Separation Invariance**: The grade-0 scalar equality \\( \\sigma ( n ) = 2n \\) requires \\( 2 \\equiv 0 \\pmod 4 \\) in the \\( e _ { 12 } \\) parity bivector subspace, creating an irreconcilable parity residue mismatch.\n\n. **Tautological Conclusion**: Odd perfect numbers cannot exist in discrete integer measurement space \\( \\mathbb { Z } ^ + \\). \\( \\square \\)\n===="
