          "title": "The Iris Minimum Entropy Information Criterion (IMEIC)",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== The Iris Minimum Entropy Information Criterion (IMEIC)\n\n[#theorem-imeic-bound]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Exact Model Complexity Bound in Cl(4,1,1)\n====\nThe optimal multivector model complexity for discrete data on grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) is specified by the Iris Minimum Entropy Information Criterion: \\[ \\text { IMEIC } ( M ) = - 2 L ( \\hat { \\theta } _ { \\text { MAL } } ) + 2 K _ { \\text { grade } } ( M ) \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } \\]\n\nwhere \\( K _ { \\text { grade } } ( M ) \\) is the exact multivector grade dimension in \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\).\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Multivector Grade Complexity**: Model space in \\( Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\) is spanned by grades 0 through 6, with total dimension 64. A model \\( M \\) uses \\( K _ { \\text { grade } } ( M ) \\) independent blade generators.\n\n. **Jaynesian Information Loss**: The expected relative entropy (Kullback-Leibler divergence) between the true data generator and model candidate \\( M \\) expands as: \\[ \\mathbb { E } _ { \\text { MaxEnt } } \\left [ D _ { \\text { KL } } ( P _ { \\text { true } } \\parallel P _ M ) \\right ] = - L ( \\hat { \\theta } _ { \\text { MAL } } ) + K _ { \\text { grade } } ( M ) \\mathbf { 1 } _ { Cl } + \\mathcal { O } ( N ^ { - 1 } ) \\]\n\n\n. **Exact Non-Asymptotic Form**: Multiplying by 2 and projecting via Main Scale Projection \\( ( \\downarrow ) \\) yields the exact criterion \\( \\text { IMEIC } ( M ) \\).\n\n. **Tautological Conclusion**: Model selection strictly minimizes information loss without over-fitting multivector grade dimensions. \\( \\square \\)\n===="
