          "id": "sec-global-simultaneous-polynomial-root-finding-and-vector-fixed-point-iteration-theorem",
          "title": "Global Simultaneous Polynomial Root-Finding and Vector Fixed-Point Iteration Theorem",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Global Simultaneous Polynomial Root-Finding and Vector Fixed-Point Iteration Theorem\n\nBeyond single-root isolation and interval subdivision, polynomial root finding can be formulated as a coupled physical/electrostatic field system where all \\( m \\) roots are evaluated simultaneously as fixed points of a multi-dimensional vector mapping \\( \\mathbf { \\Phi } ( \\mathbf { z } ) = \\mathbf { z } \\). This section establishes the theoretical framework, convergence dynamics, matrix eigenvalue connections, and fixed-point stability directives for simultaneous global polynomial root finding within the Iris number system.\n\n[#theorem-simultaneous-rootfinding-fixedpoint]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Global Simultaneous Polynomial Root-Finding and Fixed-Point Contraction Theorem\n====\nLet \\( P ( z ) = z ^ m + a _ { m - 1 } z ^ { m - 1 } + \\dots + a _ 1 z + a _ 0 \\) be a monic polynomial of degree \\( m \\ge 2 \\) over discrete rational grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\subset Cl ( 4 , 1 , 1 ) \\), with distinct simple roots \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast = ( z _ 1 ^ \\ast , z _ 2 ^ \\ast , \\dots , z _ m ^ \\ast ) ^ T \\).\n\n1. **Durand-Kerner (Weierstrass) Vector Fixed-Point Mapping**:\n   The vector iteration \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k + 1 ) } = \\mathbf { \\Phi } _ { \\text { DK } } ( \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k ) } ) \\) defined component-wise by:\n   \\[ z _ i ^ { ( k + 1 ) } = z _ i ^ { ( k ) } - \\frac { P ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } { \\prod _ { j \\neq i } ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } - z _ j ^ { ( k ) } ) } , \\quad i = 1 , 2 , \\dots , m \\]\n   possesses a fixed point at \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\) if and only if \\( P ( z _ i ^ \\ast ) = 0 \\) for all \\( i = 1 , \\dots , m \\). In an open neighborhood of simple roots, the Jacobian matrix \\( J _ { \\mathbf { \\Phi } } ( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast ) = \\mathbf { 0 } \\), guaranteeing local quadratic convergence \\( \\| \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k + 1 ) } - \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\| \\le C \\| \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k ) } - \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\| ^ 2 \\).\n\n2. **Aberth-Ehrlich Electrostatic Field Cubic Iteration**:\n   Incorporating first-derivative logarithmic field forces yields the Aberth-Ehrlich vector iteration \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k + 1 ) } = \\mathbf { \\Phi } _ { \\text { AE } } ( \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k ) } ) \\):\n   \\[ z _ i ^ { ( k + 1 ) } = z _ i ^ { ( k ) } - \\frac { \\frac { P ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } { P ' ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } } { 1 - \\frac { P ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } { P ' ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } \\sum _ { j \\neq i } \\frac { 1 } { z _ i ^ { ( k ) } - z _ j ^ { ( k ) } } } , \\quad i = 1 , 2 , \\dots , m \\]\n   In an open neighborhood of simple roots, the Jacobian and Hessian matrices vanish at \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\), proving local cubic convergence \\( \\| \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k + 1 ) } - \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\| \\le C _ 3 \\| \\mathbf { z } ^ { ( k ) } - \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\| ^ 3 \\).\n\n3. **Global Companion Matrix Eigenvalue Equivalence**:\n   All \\( m \\) roots of \\( P ( z ) \\) coincide exactly with the eigenvalues of the Frobenius companion matrix \\( \\mathbf { C } _ P \\in \\mathcal { M } _ { m \\times m } ( \\mathcal { G } _ N ) \\):\n   \\[ \\mathbf { C } _ P = \\begin{bmatrix} 0 & 0 & \\dots & 0 & - a _ 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\dots & 0 & - a _ 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\dots & 0 & - a _ 2 \\\\ \\vdots & \\vdots & \\ddots & \\vdots & \\vdots \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\dots & 1 & - a _ { m - 1 } \\end{bmatrix} \\]\n   Applying similarity transformations (QR iteration with implicit shifts) iterates \\( \\mathbf { C } _ P \\) to a block triangular Schur form fixed point \\( \\mathbf { T } \\), simultaneously isolating all real and complex root pairs without initial seed sensitivity.\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Durand-Kerner Fixed Point Verification**: Let \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast = ( z _ 1 ^ \\ast , \\dots , z _ m ^ \\ast ) \\) be the exact roots of monic \\( P ( z ) = \\prod _ { k = 1 } ^ m ( z - z _ k ^ \\ast ) \\). Substituting \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\) into \\( \\mathbf { \\Phi } _ { \\text { DK } } \\):\n   \\[ \\Phi _ { \\text { DK } , i } ( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast ) = z _ i ^ \\ast - \\frac { P ( z _ i ^ \\ast ) } { \\prod _ { j \\neq i } ( z _ i ^ \\ast - z _ j ^ \\ast ) } = z _ i ^ \\ast - \\frac { 0 } { \\prod _ { j \\neq i } ( z _ i ^ \\ast - z _ j ^ \\ast ) } = z _ i ^ \\ast \\]\n   For distinct roots, the denominator is non-zero. Thus \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\) is a fixed point. Evaluating partial derivatives \\( \\frac { \\partial \\Phi _ { \\text { DK } , i } } { \\partial z _ j } \\) at \\( \\mathbf { z } ^ \\ast \\) yields zero for all entries \\( i , j \\), establishing quadratic convergence by Taylor expansion on grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\).\n\n. **Electrostatic Field Equivalence for Aberth-Ehrlich**: The logarithmic derivative \\( \\frac { P ' ( z ) } { P ( z ) } = \\sum _ { j = 1 } ^ m \\frac { 1 } { z - z _ j ^ \\ast } \\) represents the 2D electrostatic force field generated by point charges at roots \\( z _ j ^ \\ast \\). Rewriting for target root \\( z _ i \\):\n   \\[ \\frac { P ' ( z _ i ) } { P ( z _ i ) } = \\frac { 1 } { z _ i - z _ i ^ \\ast } + \\sum _ { j \\neq i } \\frac { 1 } { z _ i - z _ j ^ \\ast } \\]\n   Approximating exact unknown roots \\( z _ j ^ \\ast \\) by current estimates \\( z _ j ^ { ( k ) } \\) yields:\n   \\[ z _ i ^ \\ast \\approx z _ i ^ { ( k ) } - \\frac { 1 } { \\frac { P ' ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } { P ( z _ i ^ { ( k ) } ) } - \\sum _ { j \\neq i } \\frac { 1 } { z _ i ^ { ( k ) } - z _ j ^ { ( k ) } } } \\]\n   Inverting numerator and denominator gives the Aberth-Ehrlich update formula. Vanishing second partial derivatives confirm cubic convergence.\n\n. **Companion Matrix Characteristic Identity**: Computing \\( \\det ( z \\mathbf { I } - \\mathbf { C } _ P ) \\) via cofactor expansion along the final column yields \\( z ^ m + a _ { m - 1 } z ^ { m - 1 } + \\dots + a _ 1 z + a _ 0 = P ( z ) \\). Eigenvalues are preserved under orthogonal QR similarity transformations \\( \\mathbf { C } _ { k + 1 } = \\mathbf { R } _ k \\mathbf { Q } _ k = \\mathbf { Q } _ k ^ T \\mathbf { C } _ k \\mathbf { Q } _ k \\), converging to upper triangular Schur fixed points containing all \\( m \\) roots on the main diagonal. \\( \\square \\)\n====\n\n[#numerical-stability-simultaneous-rootfinding]"
