          "id": "sec-derivation-of-classical-field-inertia-and-conservation-laws",
          "title": "Derivation of Classical Field Inertia and Conservation Laws",
          "contentAsciiDoc": "=== Derivation of Classical Field Inertia and Conservation Laws\n\nThis section demonstrates that classical mechanical laws—including field momentum conservation, inertia, and action-reaction pairs—are direct mathematical corollaries of the Master Field Equation.\n\n[#theorem-field-momentum-conservation-laws]\n[THEOREM]\n.Theorem: Derivation of Classical Field Momentum Laws from the Master Field Equation\n====\nIn any source-free spatial aperture region where \\( J = \\mathbf { 0 } \\), a localized self-trapped multivector field packet governed by \\( D F = \\mathbf { 0 } \\) satisfies exact field momentum conservation, yielding the classical principles of inertia, force-momentum rate, and action-reaction equality.\n\n*Proof:*\n\n. **Principle of Field Inertia**: Expanding \\( D ( D F ) = D ^ 2 F = \\Delta _ { Cl } F = \\mathbf { 0 } \\) demonstrates that a localized field packet propagating on discrete grid \\( \\mathcal { G } _ N \\) preserves its convective velocity vector \\( u \\) and field momentum \\( p = \\int _ { \\Omega } F e _ 4 \\tilde { F } \\, dV \\) along a straight spatial trajectory in the absence of external aperture flux gradients.\n\n. **Principle of Net Force**: In the presence of a non-zero source current \\( J \\neq \\mathbf { 0 } \\), the spatial divergence of the stress-energy multivector tensor \\( T = F e _ 4 \\tilde { F } \\) satisfies \\( \\nabla \\cdot T = \\text{Scalar} ( J F + F J ) \\). Integrating over a finite grid volume \\( \\Omega \\) yields the net multivector force \\( F _ { \\text{net} } = D _ t p \\).\n\n. **Action and Reaction**: For two interacting localized field packets \\( F _ 1 \\) and \\( F _ 2 \\) in a closed grid domain \\( \\Omega \\), global stress-energy conservation enforces \\( \\int _ { \\Omega } \\nabla \\cdot ( T _ 1 + T _ 2 ) \\, dV = \\mathbf { 0 } \\), establishing that \\( F _ { 12 } = - F _ { 21 } \\). \\( \\square \\)\n====\n\n[#chap-the-principle-of-least-action-and-variational-principles-in-multivector-mechanics]"
